<GuideLayout slug="do-i-need-a-permit-to-remove-a-tree">
  <p>
    In much of South Florida, yes: removing a tree often requires a permit from your city or county. But not always.
    Whether you need one depends on where you live, what kind of tree it is, how big it is, and whether it is
    dangerous. Here is how to work it out before you call anyone with a chainsaw.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1: Find out who makes the rules for your address</h2>
  <p>
    If your home is inside a city such as Miami, Fort Lauderdale, Hollywood, Pembroke Pines or Boca Raton, that city's tree
    ordinance applies. If you live in an unincorporated area, the county's rules apply. Your property tax bill or the
    county property appraiser's website shows which one you are in.
  </p>
  <p>Then check your HOA or condo rules too. They can require approval even when the city does not.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: Check the type and size of the tree</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protected and specimen trees</strong>, often large native trees such as live oaks, usually need a permit and sometimes replacement trees.</li>
    <li><strong>Trees above a minimum trunk size</strong> usually need a permit; smaller ones often don't. The size is usually measured at about 4.5 feet above the ground.</li>
    <li><strong>Invasive species</strong> such as Australian pine and Brazilian pepper are often exempt in many places, or even encouraged to be removed.</li>
    <li><strong>Dead trees</strong> are often easier to remove, sometimes with only photos or a quick inspection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 3: Is the tree dangerous?</h2>
  <p>
    Florida law gives homeowners an important exemption. Under Florida Statute 163.045, a city or county may not require a
    permit, fee or replacement trees to prune, trim or remove a tree on residential property if an ISA certified arborist
    or a Florida licensed landscape architect documents in writing that the tree presents an unacceptable risk of
    failure to people or property.
  </p>
  <p>
    That documentation matters. Keep the written assessment and photos of the tree after it is gone, because the city can
    ask for them later. The exemption applies to residential property only, and it doesn't override HOA rules.
  </p>

  <h2>What if you remove a tree without a permit?</h2>
  <p>
    Fines vary, but they can be significant, and cities often require you to plant replacement trees as well. In most
    cases the permit costs much less than the penalty.
  </p>

  <h2>Pruning and trimming</h2>
  <p>
    Normal trimming and pruning usually doesn't need a permit. Very heavy cutting can, though: removing most of the
    canopy, or "hatracking" a tree, may be treated as removal or damage under some ordinances.
  </p>

  <h2>A quick checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm whether you are in a city or an unincorporated area.</li>
    <li>Check HOA or condo approval requirements.</li>
    <li>Identify the tree species and measure the trunk.</li>
    <li>If the tree may be dangerous, get an arborist's written assessment.</li>
    <li>Apply for a permit if one is required, then schedule the work.</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="note">
    This guide is general information, not legal advice. Rules change, so confirm the current requirements with your city
    or county. Our <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit page</a> has more detail by area, and we're happy to tell you
    what's usually required for your address when you <a href="/contact-us/">request an estimate</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
